Title: hunt: avoid turn-ending stalls on passive-notify runtimes
Created: 2026-10-02
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-10-02T09:23Z MinhHaDuong created

2026-10-02T09:23Z MinhHaDuong note Probe reproduced and measured in-session: wait returns the runner report; client interrupt aborts the watch with 'Interrupted: client interrupt' and queues the author's messages; the runner survives the interrupt (pendingTurn stayed true). Evidence recorded in the ticket body.
--- body ---

## Context

Observed 2026-10-02 across three detached hunts (0920, 0988, 0916) on the Vibe
runtime: hunt's detach branch reports the handoff and ends the turn; the runner
finishes later; the completion notification is a passive stream event that cannot
start a parent turn; the session sits idle until the author prompts. The author
had to watch runners terminate and relay readiness by hand.

## Probe evidence (2026-10-02)

1. Blocking `agent.wait` holds the turn and returns the runner's final report on
   completion (240 s sleeper, clean return).
2. A client interrupt aborts the wait with `Interrupted: client interrupt`, ends
   the parent turn, and the author's messages queue — the session stays responsive.
3. The runner survives the interrupt (still `pendingTurn: true` after the parent
   turn ended) — worst case is ping retrieval, never a killed executor.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] hunt's detach branch blocks on the runner (`agent.wait`, bounded slices) when
      the author is hands-off or notifications cannot wake the parent; otherwise
      ends the turn with the ping-retrieval note.
- [ ] Interrupt semantics stated: cancels the watch, not the runner — re-attach.
- [ ] The 5.4x inline-cost rationale preserved for the interactive case.
- [ ] `make check`, `make lint`, `make check-skills-drift` pass.
